        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Quit India movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, with the strongest local effects at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, Talcher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, Midnapore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Ballia in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and, with the greatest violence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Congress working committee resolution at Wardha on 14 July 1942; launch at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> on 8 August 1942; mass arrests and local risings from August to September 1942; underground resistance and parallel governments, with leaders in prison, until 1945; release of all jailed nationalists in 1945</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, who called on the people to Do or Die; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and Maulana Azad, who were apprehensive but backed the call; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-prasad">Rajendra Prasad</a>, who supported it openly; Aruna Asaf Ali, who presided over the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Congress Committee session on 9 August; Jayaprakash Narayan and Asoka Mehta; underground leaders such as Achyut Rao Patwardhan, Sucheta Kripalani and Nana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patil">Patil</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">almost all of the Congress leadership was imprisoned, more than 100,000 people were arrested and hundreds of civilians were killed; the movement ended in 1945 with the release of the jailed nationalists, after Labour won the British election promising independence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a></span></div>
