    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> launched the movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> on 8 August 1942 with a call to Do or Die, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cripps-mission">Cripps Mission</a> failed to win Indian support for the war effort, and almost the whole Congress leadership was in prison within hours.</li><li>Without its leaders the movement took the form of strikes, boycotts, sabotage and local parallel governments, and the British answered with more than 100,000 arrests, mass fines, public flogging and the killing of hundreds of civilians.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a>, the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and some princes opposed the movement, while Viceroy Linlithgow called it by far the most serious rebellion since 1857.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Viceroy Linlithgow told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> in a telegram of 31 August 1942 that the gravity and extent of the rebellion had been concealed from the world for reasons of military security.</p></aside>
    </section>

